[medium] internal/api/handler/workflows/import.go:62 (bounded resource usage)
The new multipart import endpoint calls ctx.MultipartForm() without any route-level BodyLimit/MaxBytesReader or per-file count/size cap. Echo/net/http will parse the full multipart request and spill large parts to temp files before DecodeSeedDefinitions runs, so an authenticated admin request with very large or many files can consume unbounded disk/memory/CPU on the API host.
Suggested fix: Add an explicit upload budget for POST /workflows/import, for example Echo middleware.BodyLimit on this route plus constants for max files and max bytes per file, reject oversized fileHeader.Size values before Open, and wrap each opened part with io.LimitReader/MaxBytesReader before decoding.
Guidance: In `internal/api/handler/workflows/import.go`, add explicit upload bounds for `POST /workflows/import`. Define clear constants near the handler, for example `maxImportFiles`, `maxImportFileBytes`, and a total body limit. Register Echo `middleware.BodyLimit` only on the import route in the workflow route setup, not on the whole `/workflows` group. In the handler function that calls `ctx.MultipartForm()`, reject requests with more than `maxImportFiles` uploaded files and reject any `multipart.FileHeader` whose `Size` is greater than `maxImportFileBytes` before calling `Open()`. After `Open()`, wrap the file reader with an `io.LimitReader` or equivalent limited reader of `maxImportFileBytes + 1`, decode from that limited reader, and return a clear 413-style per-file error if the limit is exceeded. Pitfalls: do not wrap the whole import in a transaction, do not make per-definition import errors fail the entire decoded file, and do not change the exported `SeedSummary` response contract.
